# About lumos-md

lumos-md @PROJECT_VERSION@: a Markdown editor for plain notes.

## Why lumos-md

- **Your file stays your file.** lumos-md opens and saves the exact
  bytes of a file: line endings, spaces, list markers and front matter
  stay as they are. An edit changes only the text it touches. Nothing is
  reformatted when you open or save. So lumos-md works next to Obsidian,
  Git and other tools on the same notes, and a diff shows only your own
  changes.
- **Standard Markdown.** An own CommonMark and GFM parser. Its HTML
  output is byte-equal to the reference implementations, cmark and
  cmark-gfm: a note looks the same here as on GitHub and in other tools
  built on them.
- **No lost work.** A few seconds after an edit, the unsaved text goes
  to a draft file; after a crash or a power cut, the next start offers
  to recover it. When another program changes an open file, lumos-md
  loads it as one undo step, or, with unsaved changes, shows both texts
  side by side so you can merge them.
- **Private logs.** The log has one line per event, with sizes and
  counts only. It never contains the text of a note, a search pattern
  or a link.
- **One window, many tabs.** Files open in tabs of one window; a second
  start opens its files there. The tabs come back at the next start.
- **AI agents.** An AI agent can read your open files with their
  unsaved changes and show you a file or a line, through the Model
  Context Protocol (MCP). See Help, MCP Server.
- **C++ and Qt 6.** A fast native program with no web engine. The
  Markdown core is portable C++20 and has no toolkit code, so other
  toolkits and systems can follow. Packages exist for Alpine Linux and
  Debian, and a PKGBUILD for Arch Linux; Windows and macOS builds are
  planned.

## Standards

- CommonMark 0.31.2
- GitHub Flavored Markdown 0.29: tables, task lists, strikethrough,
  extended autolinks, disallowed raw HTML
- YAML (`---`) and TOML (`+++`) front matter at the start of a file
- UTF-8 text; other bytes are kept as they are
- Model Context Protocol 2025-11-25 (also 2024-11-05, 2025-03-26 and
  2025-06-18)
- XDG Base Directory paths for settings and state on Linux

